meta {
  name: Midtrans - Settlement
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/webhooks/midtrans
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "order_id": "{{order_number}}",
    "status_code": "200",
    "gross_amount": "150000.00",
    "transaction_status": "settlement",
    "payment_type": "bank_transfer",
    "fraud_status": "accept",
    "signature_key": "REPLACE_ME"
  }
}

docs {
  This is a signed payload — `signature_key` here is a placeholder and
  will be rejected (403) as-is. Real Midtrans notifications compute it
  as sha512(order_id + status_code + gross_amount + MIDTRANS_SERVER_KEY);
  for manual testing, compute that yourself against your local
  MIDTRANS_SERVER_KEY (e.g. via `php artisan tinker`) and paste the
  result in, matching order_id/gross_amount to a real local_orders row
  exactly (order_number, total_amount).

  In practice this endpoint is exercised by Midtrans's sandbox
  dashboard (simulate a real transaction end-to-end via a Snap
  checkout) rather than by hand-crafting this request — that's the
  more realistic test since it also validates the payload shape
  Midtrans actually sends. This request is here mainly as a reference
  for what that shape looks like.

  Idempotent: the same order_id + transaction_status combination is
  only ever applied once (see webhook_events table) — replaying this
  exact request is safe and won't double-send the status-change email.
}
